# Black–Litterman Model with Investor Views

This notebook incorporates subjective investor views into
market-implied equilibrium returns using the Black–Litterman framework.

import numpy as np
import pandas as pd

from src.data_loader import load_price_data, compute_returns
from src.covariance import shrinkage_covariance
from src.equilibrium import implied_equilibrium_returns
from src.black_litterman import black_litterman_posterior

tickers = ["RELIANCE.NS", "TCS.NS", "INFY.NS", "HDFCBANK.NS", "ICICIBANK.NS"]
start_date = "2019-01-01"
end_date = "2024-12-31"

prices = load_price_data(tickers, start_date, end_date)
returns = compute_returns(prices)

cov_matrix = shrinkage_covariance(returns)

n = len(tickers)
market_weights = pd.Series([1/n] * n, index=tickers)

pi = implied_equilibrium_returns(cov_matrix, market_weights)

P = np.array([[0, 1, -1, 0, 0]])
Q = np.array([[0.03]])

omega = np.array([[0.0001]])

posterior_returns = black_litterman_posterior(
    cov_matrix, pi, P, Q, omega
)

pd.DataFrame({
    "Equilibrium Returns": pi,
    "Posterior Returns": posterior_returns
})

## Key Insight

Investor views influence portfolio expectations only in proportion
to their stated confidence.

Unlike Mean–Variance optimization, Black–Litterman prevents extreme
allocations by anchoring all views to market equilibrium.
